1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:03,570
Tuesday, April sixteenth,
twenty twenty-four.

2
00:00:04,140 --> 00:00:09,270
At one of the busiest airports on Earth, passenger
jets are taxiing through water like boats.

3
00:00:09,931 --> 00:00:13,061
Over two days, more than twelve
hundred flights are cancelled.

4
00:00:13,551 --> 00:00:14,801
Highways turn into rivers.

5
00:00:15,531 --> 00:00:17,061
Cars float in the underpasses.

6
00:00:17,539 --> 00:00:23,399
This is Dubai, a city that normally gets about half as
much rain in a whole year as Phoenix, Arizona.

7
00:00:23,439 --> 00:00:26,949
That Tuesday, it got about a year and a
half of rain in a single day.

8
00:00:27,502 --> 00:00:32,652
And in Al Barsha, Ann Celestial woke up to find that
her building had no water in the taps.

9
00:00:33,142 --> 00:00:36,732
In the middle of a flood, residents were
scooping water out of the swimming pool.

10
00:00:37,335 --> 00:00:38,925
Because here's the strange thing.

11
00:00:39,395 --> 00:00:41,865
Almost every drop of water in
this city is made.

12
00:00:42,235 --> 00:00:44,365
It comes out of the sea, through machines.

13
00:00:44,955 --> 00:00:48,245
And the one thing Dubai was never built
to do was get rid of water.

14
00:00:48,840 --> 00:00:52,360
So how does a city of four and a half million
people stay alive in one of the

15
00:00:52,400 --> 00:00:54,270
hottest, driest places on the planet?

16
00:00:54,980 --> 00:00:56,290
Where does the water come from?

17
00:00:56,620 --> 00:00:59,310
What keeps the lights, and the
air conditioning, on?

18
00:00:59,700 --> 00:01:02,770
And what happens when the desert does
something no one planned for?

19
00:01:03,323 --> 00:01:05,093
The obvious answer is oil money.

20
00:01:05,383 --> 00:01:08,793
But today, oil is only about one
percent of Dubai's economy.

21
00:01:09,363 --> 00:01:11,433
The second answer is underground water.

22
00:01:11,643 --> 00:01:13,533
But the old wells are almost gone.

23
00:01:13,963 --> 00:01:15,413
So the answer is something else.

24
00:01:15,743 --> 00:01:19,253
And to find it, you have to go back to a time
when Dubai had almost nothing.

25
00:01:23,295 --> 00:01:26,855
In eighteen eighty-one, fewer than twelve
thousand people lived here,

26
00:01:26,895 --> 00:01:29,285
in a small trading town on a muddy creek.

27
00:01:29,877 --> 00:01:31,587
What it did have was pearls.

28
00:01:31,977 --> 00:01:34,957
A British survey in the early nineteen
hundreds counted three hundred and

29
00:01:34,997 --> 00:01:39,437
thirty-five pearling boats in Dubai, and almost
every man in town went to sea for

30
00:01:39,477 --> 00:01:40,387
the summer season.

31
00:01:40,957 --> 00:01:43,107
Divers dropped to the seabed
again and again,

32
00:01:43,277 --> 00:01:44,707
from sunrise to sunset.

33
00:01:45,332 --> 00:01:48,172
One of them was Mohammed bin
Abdulla Al Suwaidi,

34
00:01:48,212 --> 00:01:50,922
remembered as one of the last pearl
divers of the Emirates.

35
00:01:51,492 --> 00:01:56,322
He spent more than fifty years at sea, and died in
nineteen ninety-eight, at ninety-six.

36
00:01:57,352 --> 00:02:01,642
His grandson later left a diplomat's career
to bring pearls back, on farms.

37
00:02:02,296 --> 00:02:06,216
Then, in the nineteen twenties, Japan
started selling cultured pearls,

38
00:02:06,256 --> 00:02:07,446
grown on farms.

39
00:02:07,836 --> 00:02:11,326
They looked almost the same, and they
cost up to ten times less.

40
00:02:12,016 --> 00:02:13,926
Then came the crash of
nineteen twenty-nine.

41
00:02:14,715 --> 00:02:18,365
Within a few years, the trade that fed
the whole coast collapsed.

42
00:02:19,015 --> 00:02:21,825
A manager at Dubai's pearl museum
later put it simply:

43
00:02:22,195 --> 00:02:23,265
people got even poorer.

44
00:02:23,695 --> 00:02:24,465
It was starvation.

45
00:02:27,279 --> 00:02:32,109
Until about nineteen sixty-one, Dubai had no
clean public water supply at all.

46
00:02:32,579 --> 00:02:34,629
People drank from shallow wells and pools,

47
00:02:34,919 --> 00:02:37,389
and diseases like typhoid and
cholera were common.

48
00:02:37,948 --> 00:02:39,958
The first fix came from underground.

49
00:02:40,428 --> 00:02:44,728
In the early nineteen sixties, a water
engineer named Eric Tulloch found fresh

50
00:02:44,768 --> 00:02:47,918
groundwater at Al Awir, in the
desert outside town,

51
00:02:48,168 --> 00:02:49,858
and pipes were laid into the city.

52
00:02:50,419 --> 00:02:55,039
Around the same time, the ruler, Sheikh
Rashid bin Saeed Al Maktoum,

53
00:02:55,079 --> 00:02:57,159
borrowed money to dredge the
silted-up creek,

54
00:02:57,199 --> 00:02:59,069
so bigger ships could come in.

55
00:02:59,359 --> 00:03:00,949
Dubai bet everything on trade.

56
00:03:01,506 --> 00:03:04,786
In nineteen sixty-six, oil was
finally found offshore,

57
00:03:04,826 --> 00:03:06,776
at a field called Fateh.

58
00:03:07,086 --> 00:03:09,476
The first barrels left in
nineteen sixty-nine.

59
00:03:09,806 --> 00:03:11,636
But Dubai never had much oil.

60
00:03:12,046 --> 00:03:13,976
Its neighbor Abu Dhabi got most of it.

61
00:03:14,416 --> 00:03:16,746
So Sheikh Rashid spent the oil money fast,

62
00:03:17,016 --> 00:03:18,726
on things that would outlast it.

63
00:03:19,056 --> 00:03:23,206
And in nineteen seventy-nine, at a stretch
of empty coast called Jebel Ali,

64
00:03:23,576 --> 00:03:25,146
two of them opened in the same year.

65
00:03:25,633 --> 00:03:29,823
In February, Queen Elizabeth the Second
opened a giant new port there.

66
00:03:30,153 --> 00:03:34,203
And in June, the same site produced
Dubai's first desalinated water.

67
00:03:34,760 --> 00:03:37,690
Desalination means taking the
salt out of seawater.

68
00:03:37,800 --> 00:03:39,690
And today it's how this city drinks.

69
00:03:40,240 --> 00:03:42,850
Almost all of Dubai's tap water
comes out of the sea.

70
00:03:43,522 --> 00:03:47,722
The Jebel Ali complex alone can make about five
hundred and ninety million gallons

71
00:03:47,762 --> 00:03:49,032
of fresh water a day.

72
00:03:49,782 --> 00:03:53,872
Guinness World Records calls it the largest
single-site desalination plant on Earth.

73
00:03:54,472 --> 00:03:57,842
That's more than half of what the whole of
New York City uses in a day.

74
00:03:58,372 --> 00:03:59,262
From one site.

75
00:03:59,822 --> 00:04:01,372
Most of it is made by boiling.

76
00:04:01,982 --> 00:04:05,212
Seawater is heated with steam from the
gas power plants next door,

77
00:04:05,482 --> 00:04:08,592
it evaporates, and the vapor turns
back into fresh water.

78
00:04:09,133 --> 00:04:12,883
Which means Dubai's electricity and its drinking
water come out of the same machines.

79
00:04:13,593 --> 00:04:16,043
If the gas turbines stop, so does
most of the water.

80
00:04:16,589 --> 00:04:17,899
And the city is thirsty.

81
00:04:18,689 --> 00:04:23,499
In twenty twenty-five, Dubai used about a hundred
and ninety-four billion gallons of water.

82
00:04:24,149 --> 00:04:27,659
On its busiest day, almost five hundred
and eighty-five million.

83
00:04:28,355 --> 00:04:30,885
That's almost everything
Dubai's plants can make.

84
00:04:30,935 --> 00:04:34,765
On its hottest days, the city runs
with almost no spare water.

85
00:04:35,314 --> 00:04:36,964
And there's no going back to wells.

86
00:04:37,834 --> 00:04:40,484
Across the Emirates, the old
groundwater is running out.

87
00:04:40,954 --> 00:04:43,664
Much of it is fossil water,
thousands of years old,

88
00:04:43,954 --> 00:04:47,224
and in farming areas the water table
drops by up to five meters,

89
00:04:47,534 --> 00:04:48,944
sixteen feet, a year.

90
00:04:49,714 --> 00:04:52,324
Here, wells are now only an
emergency backup.

91
00:04:54,931 --> 00:04:56,841
Water is only half the problem.

92
00:04:57,291 --> 00:04:58,561
The other half is heat.

93
00:04:59,113 --> 00:05:04,123
In August twenty twenty-five, the Emirates hit
fifty-one point eight degrees Celsius.

94
00:05:04,673 --> 00:05:06,603
That's a hundred and
twenty-five Fahrenheit,

95
00:05:07,033 --> 00:05:08,503
hotter than Phoenix has ever been.

96
00:05:09,024 --> 00:05:10,834
But the real danger is the humidity.

97
00:05:11,584 --> 00:05:15,544
In July twenty twenty-four, the heat index in
Dubai hit around a hundred and

98
00:05:15,584 --> 00:05:17,094
forty-four degrees Fahrenheit.

99
00:05:17,604 --> 00:05:19,934
The air coming off the Gulf is
like a steam room.

100
00:05:20,424 --> 00:05:22,594
So every summer, the law steps in.

101
00:05:23,284 --> 00:05:26,044
From June fifteenth to
September fifteenth,

102
00:05:26,084 --> 00:05:30,094
outdoor work under the sun is banned from twelve
thirty to three in the afternoon.

103
00:05:30,724 --> 00:05:33,014
Companies that break it are
fined for every worker.

104
00:05:33,601 --> 00:05:35,711
For the people who build and
clean this city,

105
00:05:36,041 --> 00:05:39,091
that break is the difference between a
hard day and a deadly one.

106
00:05:39,620 --> 00:05:43,210
Everyone else lives indoors, and
indoors means air conditioning.

107
00:05:43,860 --> 00:05:48,850
On a summer afternoon, cooling can take up as much as
sixty percent of the city's peak electricity.

108
00:05:49,531 --> 00:05:53,661
In twenty twenty-five, Dubai's peak
demand passed eleven gigawatts,

109
00:05:53,911 --> 00:05:55,761
and this summer it set a new record.

110
00:05:56,291 --> 00:05:58,661
That's the output of more than
five Hoover Dams,

111
00:05:59,011 --> 00:06:01,121
and much of it goes into keeping air cold.

112
00:06:01,767 --> 00:06:04,677
So Dubai doesn't just cool
buildings one by one.

113
00:06:04,967 --> 00:06:06,297
It pipes cold to them.

114
00:06:06,819 --> 00:06:08,349
It's called district cooling.

115
00:06:09,299 --> 00:06:13,549
Giant central plants chill water and send it through
underground pipes to whole neighborhoods.

116
00:06:14,179 --> 00:06:18,319
The biggest company doing this, Empower, says
it's the largest district cooling

117
00:06:18,359 --> 00:06:19,429
provider in the world.

118
00:06:20,020 --> 00:06:22,370
In two thousand four it had
a tiny network.

119
00:06:22,940 --> 00:06:25,830
Today it's connected to about seventeen
hundred and fifty buildings,

120
00:06:26,280 --> 00:06:31,010
and one of its plants, in Business Bay, holds a
Guinness record as the biggest of its kind.

121
00:06:31,603 --> 00:06:32,933
And the power for all of it?

122
00:06:33,443 --> 00:06:35,533
Most of it still comes from natural gas.

123
00:06:36,343 --> 00:06:40,243
South of the city, in the desert, there's a
solar park with about three point nine

124
00:06:40,283 --> 00:06:43,293
gigawatts of capacity, nearly
two Hoover Dams.

125
00:06:43,803 --> 00:06:47,853
But last year, clean energy still made only
about a sixth of Dubai's power.

126
00:06:50,436 --> 00:06:51,606
Water from the sea.

127
00:06:51,996 --> 00:06:53,126
Cold from pipes.

128
00:06:53,516 --> 00:06:56,666
But there's one thing a desert
can't make at all. Food.

129
00:06:57,287 --> 00:07:00,857
The Emirates import about eighty-five to
ninety percent of their food.

130
00:07:01,287 --> 00:07:04,477
Nearly nine of every ten bites
arrive by ship or by plane.

131
00:07:05,105 --> 00:07:07,675
That's why Jebel Ali port matters so much.

132
00:07:08,005 --> 00:07:10,315
It's one of the busiest container
ports in the world,

133
00:07:10,605 --> 00:07:11,955
and it's the city's lifeline.

134
00:07:12,508 --> 00:07:14,598
Dubai is trying to grow some of its own.

135
00:07:15,228 --> 00:07:18,938
Near the new airport, there's an indoor
vertical farm called Bustanica,

136
00:07:19,208 --> 00:07:20,878
billed as the largest in the world.

137
00:07:21,368 --> 00:07:24,398
It grows about three thousand kilos
of salad greens a day,

138
00:07:24,768 --> 00:07:27,958
around six thousand pounds,
mostly for airline meals.

139
00:07:28,348 --> 00:07:30,538
It's a drop in the bucket,
but it's a start.

140
00:07:31,105 --> 00:07:32,375
And here's a strange one.

141
00:07:32,865 --> 00:07:35,115
A city in the desert also imports sand.

142
00:07:35,683 --> 00:07:37,633
Desert sand is the wrong kind.

143
00:07:38,323 --> 00:07:40,653
The wind has polished the grains
round and smooth,

144
00:07:40,863 --> 00:07:42,953
so they don't lock together in concrete.

145
00:07:43,703 --> 00:07:45,673
Builders need rough, angular sand.

146
00:07:46,246 --> 00:07:49,556
Even Palm Jumeirah, the island
shaped like a palm tree,

147
00:07:49,686 --> 00:07:50,936
wasn't made from the desert.

148
00:07:51,446 --> 00:07:55,626
It was built from about ninety-four million
cubic meters of sand dredged from the

149
00:07:55,666 --> 00:07:59,406
bottom of the Gulf, enough to pour
Hoover Dam almost forty times,

150
00:07:59,446 --> 00:08:02,316
and sprayed into place, guided by GPS.

151
00:08:03,140 --> 00:08:06,450
And on that imported, engineered
ground, the city exploded.

152
00:08:07,320 --> 00:08:11,070
In nineteen sixty-eight, Dubai had
fifty-nine thousand people.

153
00:08:11,580 --> 00:08:15,290
By the end of twenty twenty-five, it had
four point five eight million.

154
00:08:15,740 --> 00:08:17,570
More than the city of Los Angeles.

155
00:08:18,168 --> 00:08:19,558
Oil started it.

156
00:08:19,648 --> 00:08:22,258
But today the city runs on trade, flights,

157
00:08:22,668 --> 00:08:24,538
tourism, finance and property.

158
00:08:25,028 --> 00:08:27,018
On being a place people want to come to.

159
00:08:29,531 --> 00:08:31,681
Which brings us back to that
Tuesday in April.

160
00:08:32,305 --> 00:08:35,875
Dubai had built machines to make
water, machines to make cold,

161
00:08:36,165 --> 00:08:38,095
and a port to bring in everything else.

162
00:08:38,765 --> 00:08:41,295
What it had never really built was a
way to drain the rain.

163
00:08:41,848 --> 00:08:46,798
In a city where it almost never rains, there was no
network big enough to carry a storm away.

164
00:08:47,188 --> 00:08:50,578
So the water sat in the roads, the
underpasses and the basements.

165
00:08:51,175 --> 00:08:55,725
Farheen fled her ground-floor flat with her
one-year-old as the water came through the ceiling.

166
00:08:56,535 --> 00:08:58,745
"All our apartments were
ruined," she said.

167
00:08:59,284 --> 00:09:01,234
Five people died in the Emirates.

168
00:09:02,064 --> 00:09:05,264
Insurers estimated the losses at
around three billion dollars.

169
00:09:05,814 --> 00:09:09,024
And almost immediately, people
online blamed cloud seeding.

170
00:09:09,694 --> 00:09:13,494
It's true that the Emirates fly planes that fire
salt flares into clouds to squeeze

171
00:09:13,534 --> 00:09:14,624
out a bit more rain.

172
00:09:15,229 --> 00:09:18,719
But the weather service said it ran no
seeding flights during that storm.

173
00:09:19,269 --> 00:09:21,869
And seeding can only nudge clouds
that are already there,

174
00:09:21,909 --> 00:09:24,039
by maybe ten to fifteen percent.

175
00:09:24,709 --> 00:09:26,339
It can't make a storm like that.

176
00:09:26,839 --> 00:09:30,779
Scientists found signs that warming may have
made rain like this ten to forty

177
00:09:30,819 --> 00:09:33,169
percent heavier, though the
data were thin.

178
00:09:33,919 --> 00:09:36,909
A desert city now has to prepare for
floods as well as heat.

179
00:09:37,462 --> 00:09:41,282
Two months later, Dubai approved a
drainage project called Tasreef.

180
00:09:41,322 --> 00:09:44,662
Thirty billion dirhams, about
eight billion dollars,

181
00:09:44,702 --> 00:09:49,692
to raise the city's rainwater drainage capacity by
seven hundred percent by twenty thirty-three.

182
00:09:50,423 --> 00:09:54,543
At the same time, it's building one of the
world's largest seawater plants that

183
00:09:54,583 --> 00:09:58,373
filter out salt with membranes instead
of boiling it, at Hassyan.

184
00:09:58,903 --> 00:10:03,143
And by twenty thirty, the plan is to make all
desalinated water with clean energy

185
00:10:03,183 --> 00:10:04,073
and waste heat.

186
00:10:04,597 --> 00:10:06,467
So that's how Dubai stays alive.

187
00:10:07,057 --> 00:10:10,427
It makes its water from the sea, pipes
cold under its streets,

188
00:10:10,717 --> 00:10:15,307
ships in its food and even its sand, and spends
whatever it takes to keep the desert outside.

189
00:10:15,997 --> 00:10:19,147
Would you live in a city where every glass
of water starts in the sea?

190
00:10:19,637 --> 00:10:20,607
Tell me in the comments.

191
00:10:21,311 --> 00:10:24,401
And when Ann Celestial's taps ran dry
in the middle of a flood,

192
00:10:24,751 --> 00:10:27,021
it showed how thin that line really is.

193
00:10:27,611 --> 00:10:30,281
Here, even a glass of water
depends on pumps,

194
00:10:30,451 --> 00:10:32,641
pipes and power, every single day.

195
00:10:33,179 --> 00:10:36,249
If you want to see how another city
survives where it shouldn't,

196
00:10:36,359 --> 00:10:38,309
watch our story on Hong Kong next.

197
00:10:38,539 --> 00:10:42,089
And subscribe, so you don't miss how the
next place on Earth is run.
